            <ArticleTitle>Primary twin omental pregnancy: report of a rare case and literature review.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>2006.e13-5</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1016/j.fertnstert.2008.03.038</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To report a very rare case of a primary twin omental pregnancy.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="DESIGN" NlmCategory="METHODS">Case report.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="SETTING" NlmCategory="METHODS">The emergency department of a university teaching hospital.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="PATIENT(S)" NlmCategory="METHODS">A 36-year-old woman.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="INTERVENTION(S)" NlmCategory="METHODS">Partial omentectomy by laparotomy.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="MAIN OUTCOME MEASURE(S)" NlmCategory="METHODS">Laparotomy successfully performed in the case of a twin omental pregnancy.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULT(S)" NlmCategory="RESULTS">A 36-year-old woman presented with intense abdominal pain of 3 days' duration, hypotensive, with distended abdomen and signs of peritoneal irritation, and no transvaginal bleeding or evidence of topic pregnancy. Laparotomy showed normal internal genital organs and the presence of a large hemoperitoneum with a twin omental pregnancy. Partial omentectomy was performed. The patient progressed well postoperatively, and subsequent beta-hCG titer was negative.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION(S)" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Primary omental twin pregnancy is extremely rare, and when associated with acute abdomen and large hemoperitoneum, laparotomy is required for treatment.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
